==English==
{{was wotd|2024|July|1}}

===Etymology===
{{uncertain|en|Origin uncertain}}, often hypothesized to derive from the name or speech of an early Canadian minority, later broadened to denote all Canadians:<ref name="Allen">{{cite-book|author=Irving Lewis Allen|chapter=Flippity Floppity: The Semantic Inversion and Transmigration of Slurs|title=Unkind Words: Ethnic Labeling from Redskin to WASP|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Bergin & Garvey, {{w|Greenwood Publishing Group}}|year=1990|section=part I (Traditional Slurs)|pages=59 and 61–62|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/unkindwordsethni0000alle/page/59/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-89789-217-9}}.</ref>

* Since 1975, many scholars have come to think the name is from {{der|en|haw|kanaka|t=man}},<ref>{{R:DCHP|pos=n.'' & ''adj}}</ref><ref name="AHD">{{R:AHD}}</ref><ref>{{R:Collins English Dictionary|pos=n|source=Webster}}</ref> a self-appellation of indentured colonial [[canoeman|canoemen]] and Hawaiian sailors working off the Pacific Northwest, Arctic{{,}} and New England coasts, from {{cog|fr|canaque|t=indigenous Melanesian inhabitant of New Caledonia, Kanak}}; or, more likely, American [[whalers]]’ pidgin,<ref name="Dollinger">{{cite-journal|author=Stefan Dollinger|title=Towards a Fully Revised and Extended Edition of the ''Dictionary of Canadianisms on Historical Principles'' (DCHP-2): Background, Challenges, Prospects|journal=Historical Sociolinguistics and Sociohistorical Linguisics|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230417092436/https://www.let.leidenuniv.nl/hsl_shl/DCHP-2/DCHP-2/DCHP-2.htm|location=Amsterdam|publisher={{w|John Benjamins Publishing Company}} for the Department of Humanities, {{w|Leiden University}}|month=August|year=2006|volume=6|issn=1877-9069|oclc=697272245}}.</ref> then re-interpreted as {{m|en|Canadian|Can(adian)}} + a {{glossary|suffix}}.<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com|pos=n}}</ref> (More below on that [specific] putative suffix.) Compare {{cog|en|Kanak}} and {{cog|de|Kanake}}.
* Some dictionaries suggest it is derived from the first syllable of {{m|en|Canada}},<ref name="AHD"/><ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n.'' and ''adj|id=1450910864|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n.'' and ''adj}}</ref> or its {{glossary|etymon}} {{noncog|lre|kanata|t=village}}, or a related word ''kanuchsa'' meaning “villager” in either Laurentian or another [[w:Iroquoian languages|Iroquoian language]];<ref>{{cite-book|author=[[w:Bill Casselman (writer)|Bill [William Gordon] Casselman]]|chapter=Place Names|title=Casselman’s Canadian Words|location=Toronto, Ont.|publisher=[[w:Pearson plc|Copp Clark]]|year=1995|page=89|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/casselmanscanadi0000cass_i3w1/page/89/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-7730-5515-5}};</ref> with the second syllable connected to {{noncog|iu|inuk|t=man; person}},<ref>{{cite-journal|author=W. W. Schuhmacher|title=Once More ''Canuck''|editor=Ronald R. Butters|journal=[[w:American Speech|American Speech: A Quarterly of Linguistic Usage]]|location=Tuscaloosa, Ala.|publisher={{w|University of Alabama Press}} for the {{w|American Dialect Society}}|month=summer|year=1989|volume=64|issue=2|page=149|issn=0003-1283|jstor=455040|oclc=909434823}}.</ref> from {{m|en|Chinook|t=Aboriginal people of the U.S. Pacific Northwest}},<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> or another First-Nation language ending like {{m|und||-oc}}, {{m|und||-uc}}, or {{m|und||-uq}}.
* Fanciful and unlikely suggestions include {{noncog|de|[[genug]] [[von]] [[Canada]]|lit=enough of Canada}} (allegedly uttered by German mercenaries during the American War of Independence), {{noncog|fr|[[quelle]] [[canule]]|t=what a bore}} (allegedly uttered by the French during a siege of Quebec), or the surname {{m|fr|Connaught}} {{IPAchar|/ˈkɑ.nəxt/}} (supposedly a French-Canadian nickname for the Irish).<ref name="Dollinger"/>

