# {{senseid|en|Canadian person}}{{lb|en|Canada|US|informal|sometimes|derogatory}} A [[Canadian#Adjective|Canadian]] [[person#Noun|person]]; specifically {{lb|en|archaic}}, a [[French Canadian#Adjective|French Canadian]] person; a [[pea-souper]]; also {{lb|en|obsolete}} a Canadian person of other [[non-]][[English#Adjective|English]] [[descent]]. {{defdate|from early 19th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|Canadian|Canajan|Canajun|Johnny Canuck}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[Henry Cook Todd]|title=Notes upon Canada and the United States of America: In the Year MDCCCXXXV{{nb...|by a Traveller.}}|location=Toronto, Ont.|publisher={{...|Printed by}} [[w:Book publishers in Upper Canada#William Joseph Coates (1805–1879)|W[illiam] J[oseph] Coates]],{{nb...|160 King Street.}}|year=1835|section=paragraph 263|page=92|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/notesuponcanadau00todd/page/92/mode/1up|oclc=53736924|passage=Canadians are somewhat jealous of the Americans; that they are secretly manœuvering, not exactly with the inoffensive good humor of a much respected yeoman of England, {{...}} but rather after the inordinate example of {{w|Ahab}} of old, so pithily recorded by the sacred historian. Jonathan distinguishes a Dutch or French Canadian, by the term '''''Kanuk'''''.|footer={{small|“Jonathan” is a 19th-century nickname for a New Englander.}}|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:James Edward Alexander|James E[dward] Alexander]]|chapter=XV|title=L’Acadie; or, Seven Years’ Explorations in British America.{{nb...|[...] In Two Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher={{w|Henry Colburn}},{{nb...|publisher, Great Marlborough Street.}}|year=1849|volume=I|pages=272–273|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/McGillLibrary-126492-4202/page/273/mode/1up|oclc=973231654|passage=We saw a few partridges: we also met a lusty fellow in a forest road with a keg of whisky slung round him, who called to us 'Come boys and have some grog, I'm what you call a '''canuck''':' a (Canadian).|footer={{small|Referring to an English-speaking Canadian.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John G[eorge] Donkin|chapter=XIII|title=Trooper and Redskin in the Far North-West: Recollections of Life in the North-West Mounted Police, Canada, 1884–1888|location=London|publisher=[[w:Sampson Low|Sampson Low, Marston, Searle, & Rivington]]{{nb...|Limited, St. Dunstan’s House, Fetter Lane, Fleet Street, E.C.}}|year=1889|page=148|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/trooperredskinin00donkuoft/page/148/mode/1up|oclc=5755324|passage=It is a pity these Canadian militiamen spoilt the good work they had done by never-failing bluster. But for pure and unadulterated brag I will back the lower-class '''Canuck''' against the world. The Yankee is a very sucking dove compared to his northern neighbour.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Sports of field and track: Toronto 14, Rochester 11—first game|journal=w:Democrat and Chronicle|location=Rochester, N.Y.|publisher=Rochester Printing Company|date=26 August 1895|volume=63|issue=238|page=11|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/democrat-and-chronicle-canuck/149780751/|column=1|issn=1088-5153|oclc=5757784|passage=[T]he '''Canucks''' were ready with bats, hands, feet and heads, to outplay the locals at all points. They did it to a nicety. It is wonderful to contemplate the quality of ball speiled by the '''Canucks''' when their lungs are filled with Rochester air.|footer={{small|Referring to members of a Toronto baseball team.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Rowland Robinson|Rowland E[vans] Robinson]]|chapter=Forebodings of Storm|title=A Hero of Ticonderoga|location=Burlington, Vt.|publisher=Hobart J[oseph] Shanley & Co.|year=1898|page=140|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/heroticonderoga00robirich/page/140/mode/1up|oclc=7177202|passage=He's got a '''Canuck''' a-workin' for him, and I'd livser trust a wolf 'n one o' them pea-soupers.|footer={{small|Referring to a French Canadian.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Howard Angus Kennedy|chapter=The Dry Patch|title=New Canada and the New Canadians|edition=2nd|location=London|publisher=[[w:Horace Brooks Marshall, 1st Baron Marshall of Chipstead|Horace Marshall & Son]]|month=May|year=1907|year_published=October 1907|page=192|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/newcanadanewcana00kennrich/page/192/mode/1up|oclc=561081393|passage=The new-comers were a couple of farmers from Minnesota, genuine Americans from birth; wise men, with a keg of good water in their waggon. "And don't you want to be Americans any longer?" I asked. "No," said they most emphatically, "we're '''Canucks''' now."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Troops pack it away|journal=[[w:Toronto Star|Toronto Daily Star]]|location=Toronto, Ont.|publisher=The Toronto Star Limited|date=9 August 1940|page=18|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-toronto-star-canuck/149788550/|column=7|issn=0837-340X|oclc=219788467|passage=One person who realizes that the Canadian troops in England have healthy appetites is Corp. J. R. Johnstone, chief butcher of a unit. He and his 16 assistants drag from huge refrigerators eight tons of meat per day for the hungry '''Canucks'''; and every other day when rationing is less severe the butchers carve up 16 tons of lamb, beef and pork.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Larry Wood|title=[[w:Lyall Dagg|Dagg]] stays unbeaten: Scotch Cup winner decided tonight|editor=Richard L[ouis] Sanburn|journal=[[w:Calgary Herald|The Calgary Herald]]|location=Calgary, Alta.|publisher=The Southam Company|date=19 March 1964|page=18|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/calgary-herald-canuck/149779068/|column=5|issn=1197-2823|oclc=|passage=The Scottish skip {{quote-gloss|{{w|Alex F. Torrance}}}} missed a wide open takeout in the fifth leaving the '''Canucks''' another single.|footer={{small|Referring to members of the Canadian curling team.}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Richard Stursberg|title=The Secret Canadian Life of Jack Kerouac: Reading Kerouac’s Lost French Writings Reveals the Travails of a Canuck in America|journal=[[w:Maclean's|Maclean’s]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240604194610/https://www.macleans.ca/culture/books/the-secret-canadian-life-of-jack-kerouac/|location=Toronto, Ont.|publisher=Maclean-Hunter Publishing|date=12 June 2016|issn=0024-9262|oclc=1287845798|passage=[[w:Jack Kerouac|{{quote-gloss|Jack}} Kerouac]]'s writings reveal that, although celebrated as an iconic American, he thought of himself as first and foremost Canadian. In ''La Vie est d'Hommage'', he writes, "I am French Canadian. When I am angry, I often swear in French; when I dream, I often dream in French." He went on to say that "all my knowledge comes from my being French Canadian.” But as a '''Canuck''' in the United States, he felt patronized. He needed to hide his true self. Even with his friends in New York, with {{w|Allen Ginsberg}} and [[w:William S. Burroughs|William Burroughs]], he was "a completely different man. We have to live in English, it's impossible to live in French. This is the secret thought of the '''Canuck''' in America."|footer={{small|The quotations were translated from French by Stursberg.}}}}
