##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Josiah Gilbert Holland|J[osiah] G[ilbert] Holland]]|chapter=Miss Gilbert Visits the Sky, and Little Venus Takes up Her Permanent Residence there|title=Miss Gilbert’s Career: An American Story|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Charles Scribner's Sons|Charles Scribner]],{{nb...|124 Grand Street.}}; London: [[w:Sampson Low|Sampson Low, Son & Co.]]|year=1860|page=29|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/missgilbertscare00hollrich/page/29/mode/1up|oclc=273483|passage=I'll sit here and blow till he comes round with his old go-cart, and then I'll hang on to the tail of it, and try legs with that little '''Kanuck''' of his.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|title=The ‘Canuck’ horse|journal=[[w:The Boston Globe|The Boston Weekly Globe]]|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=Globe Publishing Company|date=26 January 1892|volume=XX|issue=4|page=6|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-boston-weekly-globe-canuck/149776873/|column=4|oclc=10829469|passage=Forty years ago the States south of the French part of Canada procured a large number of horses from that country. These were popularly called "'''Canucks'''," and were distinguished from the horses of the country by strongly marked characteristics. {{...}} The '''Canuck''' was thoroughly a Norman horse in all respects except size, though having more strength in proportion to size and more spirit, {{...}} '''Canucks''' in general had excessive knee action, and were natural trotters or pacers, were not built for galloping, and not inclined to take that gait; {{...}}}}

====Usage notes====
Regarding {{senseno|en|Canadian person}} (“Canadian person”), in Canada the term is not derogatory, and is considered to apply to all Canadians. When used by non-Canadians, especially in the United States, the term is often considered derogatory, particularly when applied to French Canadians in New England.

